message.content:
Lead: an owner-approved measurement, ahead of fix C (it decides an architecture). QUESTION: what fraction of allocations die inside the query (for the compiler) or the function call (for a program) that made them? If ≥80%, lifetime REGIONS (allocate in the making query's or call's region, move out only what escapes, bulk-free the rest) would remove most RC and allocation cost. If low, they wouldn't. Method: a census hook at box birth tags the innermost running query (engine) or frame (fn), and at death records whether it died before that scope ended or outlived it (escaped). Report, for cold check-cli and build-cli plus one ordinary program (std-json's tests):
(1) the % of boxes and of bytes dying in-scope vs escaping;
(2) of the escapes, the top 10 sites (answers, caches, closures, cells);
(3) the % of retains and releases spent on boxes that die in-scope.
Report only, ~2 h, no fix. Post the numbers.

